I don't quite understand what you mean since IMHO you're mixing apples  
and oranges. Technically lines and segments are equivalent - they  
differ only in the parametrization which has negligible effect on the  
speed. Technically, lines have nothing to do with ilines - one is  
using R graphics to paint on a canvas, the other is creating an object  
in iPlots. Equally, iPlots have nothing to do with R graphics.

I still have no idea what you're trying to do or what your real  
question is. The speed of drawing lines depends very heavily on the  
back-end, far more so than paining points. It is irrelevant whether  
you use segments or lines, the graphical representation is the same  
and that is where all the time is spent. therefore I have no clue why  
you are so concerned about segments. In addition, the speed depends  
heavily on the OS, aliasing settings and the opacity (no antialiasing,  
fully opaque lines are by far the fastest to draw).


> Do you know how segments is implemented in the R base package and  
> would an equally fast efficient isegments be possible for iplots  
> package?
>

